4. Earlier training data  --  NON-COMMERCIAL, NO LONGER IN THE SHIPPED WEIGHTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Versions of docs/EarLandmarker_web.onnx before this one were trained on the
corpus below, 98% of which is non-commercial by two independent routes. Those
earlier weights were research-use only. They are superseded, but the sources are
still what data/preprocessed holds, so anything you train from it inherits these
terms:

| source      | samples | licence                              | commercial use |
|-------------|---------|--------------------------------------|----------------|
| collectionB |   3,153 | iBUG: non-commercial research only   | NO             |
| collectionA |     605 | iBUG: non-commercial research only   | NO             |
| AudioEar2D  |   2,000 | annotations CC BY 4.0, IMAGES FFHQ   | NO             |
| AudioEar3D  |     112 | CC BY 4.0, provenance unverified     | unclear        |

AudioEar (Huang et al., AAAI 2023) publishes its annotations CC BY 4.0, confirmed
on its Zenodo record (https://zenodo.org/records/7592895). But AudioEar2D's 2,000
IMAGES are drawn from FFHQ, and FFHQ (https://github.com/NVlabs/ffhq-dataset) is

    Creative Commons BY-NC-SA 4.0

for the dataset, with the underlying Flickr images under licences that NVIDIA
summarises as "allow free use, redistribution, and adaptation for NON-COMMERCIAL
purposes". A permissive licence on annotations does not make the pixels they
annotate permissive, and the model was trained on the pixels.

The iBUG ear data (Zhou & Zaferiou, "Deformable Models of Ears in-the-wild",
FG 2017, https://ibug.doc.ic.ac.uk/resources/ibug-ears/) states:

    "The dataset is available for non-commercial research purposes only."

    "You agree not to reproduce, duplicate, copy, sell, trade, resell or
    exploit for any commercial purposes, any portion of the annotations and
    ANY PORTION OF DERIVED DATA."

    "You agree not to further copy, publish or distribute any portion of
    annotations of the dataset."

Identification basis: these sources use iBUG's exact 55-point scheme, verified
against the iBUG page, and collectionA's 605 samples match its documented
Collection A count.

